window.ECON_DATA={"chapters":[{"id":"23","title":"Measuring a Nation’s Income","kicker":"GDP: what is produced, how it is counted, and what it leaves out","summary":"GDP measures current domestic production. Learn the inclusion rules, classify spending correctly, and separate changes in output from changes in prices.","sections":[{"title":"One flow, two ways to measure it","body":["For the economy as a whole, total expenditure on domestic output equals total income earned from producing that output. A buyer’s payment becomes a seller’s income.","GDP is the market value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a given period. ‘Domestic’ refers to location, not the owner’s nationality.","Market prices allow different products to be added together. Both tangible goods and services count.","GDP is a flow over a period, usually a quarter or a year. Quarterly reports can be seasonally adjusted to remove predictable seasonal patterns."],"formulas":[{"label":"Accounting identity","expression":"Total expenditure on domestic output = Total income from domestic production","note":"These are two views of the same economic activity."}],"example":{"title":"Location matters","prompt":"An American-owned restaurant produces meals in France. Which country records the production in GDP?","steps":["The meals are produced inside France.","Ownership does not move that production to the United States."],"answer":"The restaurant’s production is part of French GDP."},"traps":["Do not add income and expenditure together: that would count the same production twice.","GDP follows the country of production, not the producer’s citizenship."],"source":"Chapter23.pptx, slides 3–6; Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q1–4 and Q10"},{"title":"The three inclusion tests: final, current, domestic","body":["Final goods are bought for their end use. Intermediate goods are inputs into another product; their value is already included in the final product’s price.","The same item can be final or intermediate depending on its use. Flour bought by a household for home baking is final; flour bought by a bakery for bread sold to customers is intermediate.","A resale of an existing good does not represent current production. Newly produced parts or a newly supplied dealer service can count separately.","Most unpaid household production and much illegal production are excluded because they are difficult to measure. A financial transfer alone is not production."],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Used car versus new parts","prompt":"Someone buys an old car, buys newly made replacement parts, and repairs it without pay.","steps":["The old car was produced in an earlier period, so its resale is excluded.","The new parts are current production and count.","The unpaid repair time is household work and is excluded from measured GDP."],"answer":"Count the newly produced parts, not the old car’s resale value or unpaid repair time."},"traps":["A sale this year is not automatically production this year.","Do not count both an intermediate input and the full final product.","‘Excluded from measured GDP’ does not mean an activity has no value."],"source":"Chapter23.pptx, slides 4–5; Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q5–9; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q1–2"},{"title":"Classify spending: C + I + G + NX","body":["Consumption (C): household purchases of goods and services, including durable goods such as refrigerators. A newly built house is the key household exception: it is investment.","Investment (I): new capital equipment, new structures, new housing, and changes in inventories. Buying stocks or bonds is a financial transaction, not GDP investment.","Government purchases (G): government spending on currently produced goods and services, including government employees’ services and public works. Transfer payments are excluded because no current product is exchanged for them.","Net exports (NX): exports minus imports. Exports add domestic production sold abroad. Imports are subtracted because imported goods may already appear in C, I, or G.","If a business buys last year’s unsold equipment from inventory, its equipment investment rises but inventory investment falls. Aggregate investment and current GDP are unchanged by the equipment itself."],"formulas":[{"label":"Expenditure approach","expression":"Y = C + I + G + NX","note":"Y is GDP / total expenditure on domestic output."},{"label":"Net exports","expression":"NX = Exports − Imports","note":"Net exports can be negative."}],"example":{"title":"An imported cement mixer","prompt":"An Egyptian firm buys a newly produced cement mixer from Slovakia.","steps":["Egypt’s investment rises because the firm bought capital equipment.","Egypt’s imports rise, so its net exports fall by the same amount.","The two changes offset in Egyptian GDP. Slovakia records the exported domestic production."],"answer":"Egypt: I rises, NX falls, GDP is unchanged by the machine itself. Slovakia: exports and GDP rise."},"traps":["New housing belongs in I, not C.","Unsold current output added to inventory counts as investment.","Do not subtract depreciation when the question asks for gross domestic product.","Import subtraction removes foreign production; it does not mean imports are automatically a loss of domestic output."],"source":"Chapter23.pptx, slides 7–10 including slide 10 notes; Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q11–17; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q4, Q7–8"},{"title":"Worked GDP calculation: sort before adding","body":["First classify each entry. Add the entries that belong to C, I, G, and NX. Leave out transfers and avoid subtracting depreciation for GDP.","A clear four-line setup prevents double counting and makes multiple-choice distractors easier to reject."],"formulas":[{"label":"Table 23-1 calculation","expression":"GDP = 3,311 + 1,999 + 1,975 − 32 = 7,253","note":"Use the table’s original monetary units."}],"example":{"title":"Problem Set Ch23, Table 23-1","prompt":"Use the table’s household spending, investment, government spending, exports, and imports to find GDP.","steps":["C = durable goods 1,293 + nondurable goods 1,717 + services 301 = 3,311.","I = new housing 704 + capital equipment 310 + inventory change 374 + new structures 611 = 1,999.","G = government salaries 1,422 + public works 553 = 1,975.","NX = exports 88 − imports 120 = −32.","Exclude transfer payments 777. Do not subtract depreciation 117 from GDP.","GDP = 3,311 + 1,999 + 1,975 − 32 = 7,253."],"answer":"GDP = 7,253; net exports = −32."},"traps":["Adding transfers would overstate production.","Negative net exports are valid; do not turn −32 into +32."],"source":"Problem Set Ch23.docx, Table 23-1 and Q18–19"},{"title":"Nominal GDP, real GDP, and the GDP deflator","body":["Nominal GDP values each year’s output at that year’s prices, so it changes when prices, quantities, or both change.","Real GDP uses one base year’s prices with each year’s quantities. This removes price changes and lets you compare production over time.","The GDP deflator measures the price level of current domestic production relative to the base year. In the base year, nominal GDP equals real GDP and the deflator equals 100.","Economic growth is the percentage change in real GDP. A rise in nominal GDP alone does not prove that production rose.","Slide check: with 2019 base prices of 30 and 100, 2020 quantities of 1,000 and 200 give real GDP of 50,000. For 2021, nominal GDP is 58,300 and real GDP is 52,000, so the deflator is approximately 112.1."],"formulas":[{"label":"Nominal GDP","expression":"Nominal GDP in year t = Σ(price in year t × quantity in year t)","note":"Use current prices and current quantities."},{"label":"Real GDP","expression":"Real GDP in year t = Σ(base-year price × quantity in year t)","note":"Keep the price column fixed."},{"label":"GDP deflator","expression":"GDP deflator = (Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP) × 100","note":"A deflator of 120 means the price level is 20% above its base-year level, not 120% above it."},{"label":"Recover real GDP","expression":"Real GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ (GDP deflator ÷ 100)","note":"Divide by 1.20 when the deflator is 120."},{"label":"Real GDP growth","expression":"Growth rate = [(Real GDPnew − Real GDPold) ÷ Real GDPold] × 100%","note":"The old value is the denominator."}],"example":{"title":"Goat milk and bananas: Year 1 is the base year","prompt":"Year 1 quantities: 200 milk and 80 bananas; prices: 2.00 and 3.00. Year 2 quantities: 180 and 90; prices: 2.40 and 3.20. Year 3 quantities: 160 and 100; prices: 2.50 and 3.10.","steps":["Year 1 nominal and real GDP = 200 × 2.00 + 80 × 3.00 = 640.","Year 2 nominal GDP = 180 × 2.40 + 90 × 3.20 = 720.","Year 2 real GDP = 180 × 2.00 + 90 × 3.00 = 630.","Year 3 nominal GDP = 160 × 2.50 + 100 × 3.10 = 710.","Year 3 real GDP = 160 × 2.00 + 100 × 3.00 = 620.","Year 3 deflator = (710 ÷ 620) × 100 ≈ 114.5.","From Year 1 to Year 2, real growth = [(630 − 640) ÷ 640] × 100 ≈ −1.56%, even though nominal GDP rose."],"answer":"Nominal GDP: 640, 720, 710. Real GDP: 640, 630, 620. Year 3 deflator: approximately 114.5."},"traps":["Real GDP fixes prices, not quantities.","A larger nominal GDP may be caused entirely by higher prices.","The deflator is an index level; inflation is a percentage change between index levels."],"source":"Chapter23.pptx, slides 11–17, including slide 12 image table; Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q20–25 and Q28–30; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q3, Q5–6 and Q9"},{"title":"GDP and well-being: useful, but incomplete","body":["Real GDP per person is a useful average indicator of material living standards. Higher average production can support more goods and services per person.","GDP does not directly measure leisure, unpaid household or volunteer work, environmental quality, or how income is distributed.","An increase in GDP does not guarantee that every person is better off. Distinguish an average from the experience of every household."],"formulas":[{"label":"Average output","expression":"Real GDP per person = Real GDP ÷ Population","note":"An average, not a statement about equal distribution."}],"example":{"title":"More output, uneven benefits","prompt":"GDP per person rises, but all of the additional income goes to a small group.","steps":["Average measured output rises.","GDP does not show how that output or income is distributed."],"answer":"The average can improve without every household’s living standard improving."},"traps":["Do not describe GDP as a complete measure of happiness or welfare.","Per-person GDP and total GDP answer different questions."],"source":"Chapter23.pptx, slides 18–20; Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q22 and Q26–27"}],"questions":[{"id":"ch23-1","chapter":"23","prompt":"A household buys an old car, buys newly produced replacement parts, and repairs it without pay. Which item counts in current GDP?","options":["The old car’s full resale price","The newly produced replacement parts","The unpaid repair time","All three items"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The parts are current production. The car was produced earlier, and unpaid household work is excluded from measured GDP.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q9"},{"id":"ch23-2","chapter":"23","prompt":"An American-owned restaurant produces meals in France. Where is that production included?","options":["Only in US GDP","Only in French GDP","In both countries’ GDP","In neither country’s GDP"],"correct":1,"explanation":"GDP follows the location of production, regardless of the business owner’s nationality.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q10"},{"id":"ch23-3","chapter":"23","prompt":"A household purchases a newly built home. Which GDP component records it?","options":["Consumption","Investment","Government purchases","Net exports"],"correct":1,"explanation":"New housing is investment, even when a household purchases it. Household durable goods such as refrigerators are generally consumption.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q13–14"},{"id":"ch23-4","chapter":"23","prompt":"An Egyptian firm imports a cement mixer from Slovakia. What happens in Egypt because of this purchase?","options":["Investment rises, net exports fall, and GDP is unchanged by the machine itself","Investment and GDP both fall","Investment and net exports both rise","Only GDP rises"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The capital purchase raises I, while the imported machine raises imports and lowers NX by the same amount. The foreign production is therefore excluded from Egyptian GDP.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q17"},{"id":"ch23-5","chapter":"23","prompt":"C = 400, I = 150, G = 200, exports = 80, and imports = 60. What is GDP?","options":["690","710","770","830"],"correct":2,"explanation":"GDP = 400 + 150 + 200 + (80 − 60) = 770.","source":"Adapted from Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q8"},{"id":"ch23-6","chapter":"23","prompt":"Year 2 output is 180 units of milk at 2.40 each and 90 bananas at 3.20 each. What is Year 2 nominal GDP?","options":["630","640","710","720"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Use Year 2 prices: 180 × 2.40 + 90 × 3.20 = 432 + 288 = 720. Using base-year prices would calculate real GDP instead.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q28"},{"id":"ch23-7","chapter":"23","prompt":"Nominal GDP is 710 and real GDP is 620. Approximately what is the GDP deflator?","options":["87.3","114.5","120.6","14.5"],"correct":1,"explanation":"(710 ÷ 620) × 100 ≈ 114.5. This is an index about 14.5% above the base-year price level.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q30"},{"id":"ch23-8","chapter":"23","prompt":"Nominal GDP rises from 75 billion to 100 billion while the GDP deflator rises from 120 to 125. Which statement is correct?","options":["Real output rises and the price level rises","Real output rises and the price level falls","Real output falls and the price level rises","Real output and the price level both fall"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Old real GDP = 75 ÷ 1.20 = 62.5 billion. New real GDP = 100 ÷ 1.25 = 80 billion. Real output rises, and the higher deflator shows that the price level also rises.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch23.docx, Q25"}]},{"id":"24","title":"Measuring the Cost of Living","kicker":"CPI, inflation, purchasing power, and real interest","summary":"Build a fixed-basket price index, calculate inflation with the old index in the denominator, and adjust money amounts for changes in purchasing power.","sections":[{"title":"Build the CPI from a fixed basket","body":["The consumer price index tracks the overall cost of goods and services bought by a typical consumer. Its purpose is to follow changes in the cost of living.","The calculation has five steps: fix the basket, collect prices, calculate each year’s basket cost, choose a base year and calculate the index, then calculate inflation.","Keep basket quantities fixed. Only prices change in the basic CPI calculation. The base-year CPI equals 100.","Handwritten slide example: fix the basket at 10 loaves, 5 litres of milk, 4 kg of chicken, and 50 litres of gasoline. The 2025 prices are KWD 0.250, 0.450, 1.500, and 0.105; the 2026 prices are KWD 0.300, 0.500, 1.650, and 0.105. Basket costs are KWD 16.00 and 17.35, giving a 2026 CPI of (17.35 ÷ 16) × 100 = 108.44."],"formulas":[{"label":"Basket cost","expression":"Basket cost in year t = Σ(fixed basket quantity × price in year t)","note":"The same quantities are priced in each year."},{"label":"Consumer price index","expression":"CPI in year t = (Basket cost in year t ÷ Basket cost in base year) × 100","note":"A CPI of 130 means the basket costs 30% more than in the base year."}],"example":{"title":"The slides’ lamb-and-chicken basket","prompt":"The basket contains 10 lb of lamb and 20 lb of chicken. The 2019 base-year basket costs 120. In 2020 both prices are 5 per lb. In 2021 lamb costs 9 and chicken costs 6.","steps":["2020 basket cost = 10 × 5 + 20 × 5 = 150.","2020 CPI = (150 ÷ 120) × 100 = 125.","2021 basket cost = 10 × 9 + 20 × 6 = 210.","2021 CPI = (210 ÷ 120) × 100 = 175."],"answer":"CPI: 2019 = 100; 2020 = 125; 2021 = 175."},"traps":["Do not update the quantities when calculating a basic fixed-basket CPI.","Do not confuse a basket’s monetary cost with its index number."],"source":"Chapter24.pptx, slides 3–9, including handwritten slides 4–5; Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q1–6; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q10 and Q12–13"},{"title":"Inflation is a percentage change, not an index-point change","body":["Inflation is the percentage increase in a price index from the preceding period. Divide by the preceding period’s index, not the new index.","The same increase in index points can imply different inflation rates. An increase from 80 to 100 is 25%; from 100 to 120 it is 20%; from 150 to 170 it is about 13.33%.","To work backwards from a new index and an inflation rate, divide by one plus the inflation rate expressed as a decimal.","In the handwritten basket example, the 2025 base-year CPI is 100 and the 2026 CPI is 108.44. Inflation is therefore approximately 8.44%."],"formulas":[{"label":"Inflation rate","expression":"Inflation = [(CPInew − CPIold) ÷ CPIold] × 100%","note":"Use the old CPI in the denominator."},{"label":"Recover the earlier index","expression":"CPIold = CPInew ÷ (1 + inflation rate as a decimal)","note":"24% becomes 0.24."}],"example":{"title":"125 to 175 is not 50% inflation","prompt":"The basket example gives a CPI of 125 in 2020 and 175 in 2021. Find the inflation rate.","steps":["The index rises by 175 − 125 = 50 points.","Divide the change by the old index: 50 ÷ 125 = 0.40.","Convert to a percentage: 0.40 × 100 = 40%."],"answer":"2020–2021 inflation = 40%."},"traps":["175 − 125 = 50 index points, not a 50% inflation rate.","Use consistent periods: the base-year comparison and the one-year inflation rate are different questions."],"source":"Chapter24.pptx, slides 5–6 and 9; Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q5–8; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q11 and Q16"},{"title":"CPI versus the GDP deflator","body":["CPI asks what happens to the price of the consumer’s basket, including imported consumer goods.","The GDP deflator covers goods and services produced domestically, including domestically produced capital goods. It excludes imported production.","CPI uses a fixed basket in the basic calculation. The GDP deflator reflects the composition of current production.","For price-change questions, check two things: Is it a consumer purchase? Is it produced domestically?"],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Three price changes","prompt":"Compare a domestic café drink, a domestically made industrial tractor, and imported consumer jeans.","steps":["Domestic café drink: consumer good and domestic production → both CPI and GDP deflator.","Domestic industrial tractor: capital good and domestic production → GDP deflator only.","Imported consumer jeans: consumer basket but foreign production → CPI only."],"answer":"Domestic consumer good: both. Domestic capital good: deflator only. Imported consumer good: CPI only."},"traps":["‘Capital goods’ are not automatically excluded from the GDP deflator; domestic capital goods are included.","‘Imports’ enter CPI only when relevant to consumers’ purchases; the claim is not that every imported item is in the consumer basket."],"source":"Chapter24.pptx, slides 13–15; Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q2 and Q15; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q17"},{"title":"Why CPI can overstate increases in living costs","body":["Substitution bias: consumers switch toward products that become relatively cheaper, but a fixed basket does not fully reflect that response.","New goods: new products increase choice and can make a given amount of money more valuable. A fixed basket misses some of this benefit.","Unmeasured quality change: a better product can deliver more value for the same price. Statistical adjustments are imperfect because quality is difficult to measure.","The chapter’s three problems tend to make measured CPI inflation overstate the rise in the cost of maintaining a given standard of living."],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Rice becomes more expensive","prompt":"Consumers can substitute potatoes for rice. Rice prices rise, so consumers buy less rice and more potatoes.","steps":["The fixed basket still contains its original rice quantity.","It captures the rice price rise without fully capturing consumers’ cheaper substitution."],"answer":"The fixed-basket measure overstates the cost increase compared with consumers’ ability to substitute."},"traps":["Substitution bias does not mean CPI ignores prices; it means the fixed quantities miss part of the response to relative prices.","An unchanged price for an improved product can still represent better value."],"source":"Chapter24.pptx, slides 10–12; Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q11–14; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q14"},{"title":"Compare money across years and understand indexation","body":["Money amounts from different years cannot be compared fairly without accounting for price levels. Convert both amounts into the purchasing power of the same year.","Multiply the earlier amount by the target-year CPI divided by the earlier-year CPI. This adjusts the monetary amount, not the underlying quantity of goods.","Indexation means an amount is automatically adjusted for inflation by law or contract, such as an indexed wage or benefit.","Handwritten slide check: income of 50,000 when CPI is 80 requires 50,000 × (120 ÷ 80) = 75,000 when CPI is 120 to buy the same amount of goods and services."],"formulas":[{"label":"Convert a money amount","expression":"Amount in target-year money = Earlier amount × (CPItarget ÷ CPIearlier)","note":"Both CPIs must use the same index series and base."}],"example":{"title":"Which salary has the greatest purchasing power?","prompt":"Annual salaries are 12,000 in 1970, 24,000 in 1980, and 36,000 in 1990. The corresponding CPIs are 40, 60, and 100.","steps":["Express all salaries in 1990 purchasing power.","1970: 12,000 × (100 ÷ 40) = 30,000.","1980: 24,000 × (100 ÷ 60) = 40,000.","1990: 36,000 × (100 ÷ 100) = 36,000."],"answer":"Purchasing power is highest in 1980 and lowest in 1970."},"traps":["The largest nominal salary need not be the largest real salary.","To move an old amount into a higher-price target year, the conversion factor should generally exceed 1."],"source":"Chapter24.pptx, slides 16–18, including handwritten slide 17; Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q16–19; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q18–19"},{"title":"Nominal interest counts money; real interest counts purchasing power","body":["The nominal interest rate describes growth in the number of monetary units in an account. The real interest rate describes growth in what that money can buy.","For this course’s calculations, subtract inflation from nominal interest. This is the standard approximation used in the slides and problem sets.","If inflation exceeds nominal interest, the real interest rate is negative: the balance can rise in money terms while purchasing power falls.","Handwritten slide example: a KWD 10,000 deposit at 4% nominal interest becomes KWD 10,400. With 2% inflation, the course’s real rate is approximately 2%; with 7% inflation, it is approximately −3%."],"formulas":[{"label":"Course real-interest calculation","expression":"Real interest rate ≈ Nominal interest rate − Inflation rate","note":"Use percentage points consistently. The course writes this as r = i − π."},{"label":"Rearrange","expression":"Inflation rate ≈ Nominal interest rate − Real interest rate","note":"Useful when the question supplies nominal and real rates."}],"example":{"title":"Find inflation first, then real interest","prompt":"CPI rises from 225 to 234. The nominal interest rate is 6.5%.","steps":["Inflation = [(234 − 225) ÷ 225] × 100 = 4%.","Real interest ≈ 6.5% − 4% = 2.5%."],"answer":"Inflation is 4%; the course’s real-interest answer is 2.5%."},"traps":["Do not subtract CPI index levels from an interest rate. Convert the index change into an inflation rate first.","Positive nominal interest does not guarantee positive real interest.","Keep the course approximation distinct from an exact compounded purchasing-power calculation."],"source":"Chapter24.pptx, slides 19–21, including handwritten slide 20; Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q20–28; Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q15"}],"questions":[{"id":"ch24-1","chapter":"24","prompt":"A fixed basket costs 200 in the base year and 260 this year. What is this year’s CPI?","options":["30","110","120","130"],"correct":3,"explanation":"CPI = (260 ÷ 200) × 100 = 130. The basket is 30% more expensive than in the base year.","source":"Adapted from Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q13"},{"id":"ch24-2","chapter":"24","prompt":"CPI rises from 120 to 132. What is the inflation rate?","options":["9.09%","10%","12%","32%"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Inflation = [(132 − 120) ÷ 120] × 100 = 10%. The change is 12 index points but 10%.","source":"Adapted from Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, Q11"},{"id":"ch24-3","chapter":"24","prompt":"Which index increase represents the greatest inflation rate?","options":["80 to 100","100 to 120","150 to 170","All three are the same"],"correct":0,"explanation":"20 ÷ 80 = 25%, compared with 20% and about 13.33%. Equal point changes do not imply equal percentage changes.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q7"},{"id":"ch24-4","chapter":"24","prompt":"CPI is 128.96 after a year of 24% inflation. What was the earlier CPI?","options":["104","104.96","152.96","159.91"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The new index equals the old index × 1.24. Therefore the old index is 128.96 ÷ 1.24 = 104.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q8"},{"id":"ch24-5","chapter":"24","prompt":"The price of imported jeans bought by domestic consumers rises. Which index is directly affected?","options":["GDP deflator only","CPI only","Both CPI and GDP deflator","Neither index"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The jeans enter consumer purchases, so they can affect CPI. They are produced abroad, so their price is excluded from the domestic GDP deflator.","source":"Adapted from Chapter24.pptx, slides 14–15"},{"id":"ch24-6","chapter":"24","prompt":"When rice becomes relatively expensive, consumers substitute cheaper potatoes. A fixed CPI basket misses part of that response. What is the problem called?","options":["Indexation","Diminishing returns","Substitution bias","Unmeasured investment"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Substitution bias occurs because fixed basket quantities do not fully capture a shift toward relatively cheaper products, tending to overstate the cost-of-living increase.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q12–14"},{"id":"ch24-7","chapter":"24","prompt":"A salary is 24,000 when CPI is 60. What is its equivalent when CPI is 100?","options":["14,400","24,000","36,000","40,000"],"correct":3,"explanation":"24,000 × (100 ÷ 60) = 40,000. The target CPI goes in the numerator.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q17"},{"id":"ch24-8","chapter":"24","prompt":"CPI rises from 225 to 234 while nominal interest is 6.5%. Using the course formula, what is real interest?","options":["2.5%","4.0%","6.76%","10.5%"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Inflation = (9 ÷ 225) × 100 = 4%. Real interest ≈ 6.5% − 4% = 2.5%.","source":"Adapted from Problem Set Ch24.docx, Q26"}]},{"id":"25","title":"Production and Growth","kicker":"Why living standards grow","summary":"Productivity explains much of the difference in long-run living standards. Learn to measure output per worker or labor-hour, distinguish the four productivity determinants, and explain why capital accumulation raises income but faces diminishing returns.","sections":[{"title":"1. Living standards and productivity","body":["Real GDP per person describes the average level of economic prosperity. Its growth rate describes how quickly that average is improving. Neither the total size of an economy nor output growth alone tells you the average living standard.","Productivity is output per unit of labor. Read the denominator carefully: output per worker and output per labor-hour are different measures. Total labor-hours equal workers multiplied by hours worked per worker.","Workers who can produce more goods and services per hour support higher real incomes over time. Output can fall while productivity rises if labor input falls by an even larger percentage.","Class-example figures from slides 3–5: for the US, $22.57 trillion = $22,570,000 million, so $22,570,000 million / 340.1 million people ≈ $66,362.83 per person. For India, $3.48 trillion = $3,480 billion, so $3,480 billion / 1.45 billion people = $2,400 per person. These are figures used in the class examples, not newly verified current country statistics."],"formulas":[{"label":"Real GDP per person","expression":"Real GDP per person = real GDP / population","note":"Convert GDP and population to compatible units before dividing."},{"label":"Output per worker","expression":"Productivity = real output / number of workers","note":"Use this when labor is measured as workers."},{"label":"Output per labor-hour","expression":"Productivity = real output / total labor-hours","note":"Total labor-hours = workers × hours per worker."},{"label":"Percentage change","expression":"Growth rate = [(new value − old value) / old value] × 100%","note":"Compute productivity first, then its growth rate."}],"example":{"title":"Less total output, higher productivity","prompt":"A firm initially produces 30,000 units using 600 labor-hours. Later it produces 28,800 units using 480 labor-hours. What happens to productivity?","steps":["Initial productivity = 30,000 / 600 = 50 units per labor-hour.","Later productivity = 28,800 / 480 = 60 units per labor-hour.","Productivity growth = (60 − 50) / 50 × 100 = 20%."],"answer":"Productivity rises by 20%, even though total output falls by 4%."},"traps":["Do not divide by population when the question asks for productivity per labor-hour.","Do not treat total GDP as GDP per person.","A fall in total production does not necessarily mean a fall in productivity."],"source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 3–5, 7 and 23; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 1–5. Productivity numerical example is new practice."},{"title":"2. The four productivity determinants","body":["Physical capital is the equipment and structures used in production: machines, factory buildings and business computers. These are produced inputs that help workers produce more.","Human capital is the knowledge and skills embodied in people through education, training and experience. Training belongs here, rather than under physical capital.","Natural resources are inputs supplied by nature, such as land and mineral deposits. Technological knowledge is society's understanding of useful production methods. Knowing a better method differs from an individual worker learning to apply it.","Compare inputs per worker when explaining productivity. An economy can add machines and workers together without increasing the machines available to each worker."],"formulas":[{"label":"Determinants per worker","expression":"K/L, H/L, N/L and A","note":"K = physical capital; H = human capital; N = natural resources; L = labor; A = technology."}],"example":{"title":"Classify the input","prompt":"A bakery buys a commercial oven, trains its bakers and adopts a newly discovered production technique. Which determinants improve?","steps":["The oven is physical capital.","The bakers' acquired skills are human capital.","The new production method is technological knowledge."],"answer":"Physical capital, human capital and technology can each raise output per worker."},"traps":["Human capital is not the number of workers or hours worked.","A machine is physical capital; learning to use it is human capital.","Natural resources are helpful inputs, but the course does not treat them as the only source of prosperity."],"source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 7–9 and 20–23; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 6–10."},{"title":"3. Production functions: scale versus diminishing returns","body":["A production function describes how labor, physical capital, human capital and natural resources combine with technology to produce output.","Constant returns to scale means that multiplying all inputs by the same factor multiplies output by that factor. Doubling all inputs doubles output, holding technology fixed.","Diminishing returns to capital asks a different question: increase physical capital while holding the other inputs fixed. Output rises, but each additional unit of capital adds less output than the previous unit.","On a graph with capital per worker on the horizontal axis and output per worker on the vertical axis, diminishing returns produce an upward-sloping curve that becomes flatter. Constant returns to scale and diminishing returns to one input can both hold."],"formulas":[{"label":"Aggregate production","expression":"Y = A F(L, K, H, N)","note":"A higher A allows more output from the same inputs."},{"label":"Constant returns to scale","expression":"xY = A F(xL, xK, xH, xN)","note":"All inputs change together; technology is fixed."},{"label":"Output per worker","expression":"Y/L = A F(1, K/L, H/L, N/L)","note":"This form follows from constant returns to scale."}],"example":{"title":"Two claims that can both be true","prompt":"A workshop doubles its workers and all other inputs. Separately, it adds a second machine while keeping its workers and other inputs fixed.","steps":["With constant returns to scale, doubling every input doubles output.","Adding only a machine raises capital per worker.","With diminishing returns to capital, the second machine adds output, but less than the first comparable machine did."],"answer":"Scaling every input is different from increasing only capital."},"traps":["Do not interpret constant returns to scale as constant returns to physical capital alone.","Diminishing returns means a smaller additional gain, not necessarily falling total output.","A flatter production curve does not mean extra capital produces no output."],"source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 8–9, 14–15 and 24; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 12–15 and 22."},{"title":"4. Saving, capital accumulation and the catch-up effect","body":["Saving allows resources to be used for investment in new productive capital. The tradeoff is less consumption now in exchange for more production and consumption possibilities later.","A higher saving rate can increase capital per worker and raise the level of productivity and income. Because capital has diminishing returns, this capital-deepening process does not permanently raise the growth rate in the course's model.","The catch-up effect describes why a capital-poor country can grow faster than a capital-rich country when other conditions are comparable: extra capital has a larger payoff where capital per worker is initially scarce.","Catch-up is a possibility, not a guarantee that every poor country grows quickly. Institutions, education and other productivity determinants also matter."],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Level versus growth rate","prompt":"A policy permanently raises the saving rate. What should the long-run claim be?","steps":["Resources shift from present consumption toward investment.","Capital per worker rises and productivity increases during the transition.","As capital accumulates, diminishing returns reduce the extra gain from further accumulation."],"answer":"A higher long-run income level, rather than a permanently higher growth rate caused by saving alone."},"traps":["Higher saving does not mean higher consumption immediately.","Distinguish a higher level of income from a permanently higher growth rate.","Poorer countries do not catch up automatically regardless of policy and institutions."],"source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 14, 22 and 24; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 11–15."},{"title":"5. Policies that strengthen productivity","body":["Saving incentives and investment from abroad can increase the capital available to domestic workers. Foreign direct investment is foreign-owned and foreign-operated capital; foreign portfolio investment uses foreign financing with domestic operation.","Education and training increase human capital, while health and nutrition help workers use their abilities effectively. Education has an opportunity cost because time spent studying is time not used for other activities.","Secure property rights, reliable courts and political stability encourage people to invest because they are more likely to retain the benefits. Corruption and fraud weaken that incentive.","Outward-oriented policies support trade and international integration. Research and development, including incentives such as patents, can improve technology, which drives sustained long-run advances in living standards."],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Match the policy to the channel","prompt":"A government funds vocational training, protects investors' property rights and supports research. How can these measures raise output per worker?","steps":["Vocational training raises human capital per worker.","Property-rights protection strengthens incentives to accumulate productive capital.","Research can improve technological knowledge."],"answer":"Identify the productivity determinant or investment incentive affected; naming a policy alone is not the explanation."},"traps":["Foreign direct and portfolio investment differ in ownership and operation, not simply in whether money crosses borders.","Import restrictions are inward-oriented policies.","Do not ignore the opportunity cost of education."],"source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 13 and 16–18, 21–24; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 16–17 and 19–21."},{"title":"6. Population growth: competing effects","body":["Faster population growth can stretch natural resources and dilute capital if the capital stock does not rise quickly enough to maintain capital per worker. More total workers alone does not ensure more output per worker.","A rapidly growing population can also place pressure on the education system, making it harder to maintain educational resources per person.","There is a potential positive channel too: a larger population can include more scientists, engineers and inventors, contributing to technological progress. For an exam explanation, name the channel rather than asserting that population growth is always good or always bad."],"formulas":[{"label":"Capital dilution","expression":"If L rises faster than K, K/L falls","note":"The relevant comparison is capital relative to labor."}],"example":{"title":"More machines, less capital per worker","prompt":"Capital rises from 200 to 220 units while the number of workers rises from 100 to 125. What happens to capital per worker?","steps":["Initial K/L = 200/100 = 2.","New K/L = 220/125 = 1.76.","Capital grows by 10%, but labor grows by 25%."],"answer":"Capital per worker falls despite an increase in the total capital stock."},"traps":["Total capital growth is not sufficient to establish growth in capital per worker.","Do not omit the possible innovation channel.","GDP per worker and total GDP need not move together."],"source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 18–19 and 22; Problem Set Ch25.docx, question 18. Numerical example is new practice."}],"questions":[{"id":"ch25-q1","chapter":"25","prompt":"Three workers each work 8 hours and produce 1,200 units. What is productivity per labor-hour?","options":["400 units","150 units","50 units","1,200 units"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Total labor-hours are 3 × 8 = 24, so productivity is 1,200/24 = 50 units per labor-hour.","source":"Problem Set Ch25.docx, question 3; adapted numbers."},{"id":"ch25-q2","chapter":"25","prompt":"A worker's ability to operate a machine improves after training. Which productivity determinant directly increases?","options":["Natural resources","Human capital","Physical capital","The number of workers"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Training adds skills embodied in the worker, which is human capital. The machine itself is physical capital.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 7 and 16; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 8–10."},{"id":"ch25-q3","chapter":"25","prompt":"With constant returns to scale, labor, physical capital, human capital and natural resources each rise by 20%, while technology stays fixed. Output will:","options":["Rise by 20%","Rise by less than 20% because capital has diminishing returns","Remain unchanged","Rise by 80%"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Constant returns to scale concerns changing every input by the same proportion. Output changes by that proportion too. Diminishing returns to capital holds the other inputs fixed.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 8–9; Problem Set Ch25.docx, question 22."},{"id":"ch25-q4","chapter":"25","prompt":"Why does the capital-per-worker versus output-per-worker curve become flatter?","options":["Extra capital necessarily reduces total output","Technology gets worse whenever capital rises","Every extra machine adds the same output","Additional capital yields progressively smaller output gains, holding other inputs fixed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The flatter curve represents diminishing marginal returns to capital: output still rises, but by smaller amounts.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 14–15; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 12–15."},{"id":"ch25-q5","chapter":"25","prompt":"In the course's growth model, a permanently higher saving rate leads in the long run to:","options":["Lower productivity and higher consumption immediately","A higher income level, but not a permanently higher growth rate from saving alone","A permanently accelerating growth rate","No change in capital per worker"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Saving supports capital accumulation and a higher income level. Diminishing returns make the growth effect of capital accumulation fade.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slide 14; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 11 and 14."},{"id":"ch25-q6","chapter":"25","prompt":"Other things equal, extra capital can have a bigger effect in a capital-poor economy because:","options":["Poor countries always have better institutions","Labor does not matter in poorer countries","Capital has a higher marginal payoff when capital per worker is initially low","All poor countries must grow at the same rate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"This is the catch-up effect. It depends on comparable conditions and does not guarantee rapid growth in every poor country.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 14 and 24."},{"id":"ch25-q7","chapter":"25","prompt":"A foreign company builds, owns and operates a domestic factory. This is:","options":["Foreign direct investment","Foreign portfolio investment","A reduction in domestic physical capital","Human capital only"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Foreign ownership and operation identify foreign direct investment. Portfolio investment supplies foreign financing while domestic residents operate the investment.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slide 16; Problem Set Ch25.docx, questions 16–17."},{"id":"ch25-q8","chapter":"25","prompt":"Which statement best captures the possible effects of population growth?","options":["It can only improve productivity","It can only reduce technological progress","It guarantees higher capital per worker","It can dilute capital, while also increasing the number of potential inventors"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The slides describe resource pressure and capital dilution, but also a potential increase in innovation from more scientists, engineers and inventors.","source":"Chapter25.pptx, slides 18–19."}]},{"id":"26","title":"Saving, Investment and the Financial System","kicker":"Follow the funds","summary":"The financial system connects saving with investment. Master the saving identities first, then explain loanable-funds diagrams by identifying the curve that shifts and the resulting changes in the interest rate and investment.","sections":[{"title":"1. Markets, intermediaries, stocks and bonds","body":["The financial system channels funds from savers toward borrowers who finance investment. Financial markets connect them directly; financial intermediaries stand between them.","A bond is debt: its buyer lends to the issuer and becomes a creditor. A stock represents partial ownership, so selling stock is equity finance. Stockholders' returns depend on the firm's success.","Banks accept deposits and make loans. Mutual funds sell shares and use the proceeds to buy portfolios of assets, making diversification accessible to small savers.","For bonds, compare term, credit risk and tax treatment. Other things equal, greater default risk or a longer term generally requires a higher interest rate; tax-exempt interest can allow a lower quoted rate. These comparisons require holding other characteristics constant."],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"How does the firm obtain the funds?","prompt":"A company can issue a bond, issue new stock or borrow from a bank. Classify each arrangement.","steps":["Bond issue: debt finance through a financial market.","Stock issue: equity finance through a financial market.","Bank loan: borrowing through a financial intermediary."],"answer":"Bonds create creditors; shares create owners; a bank intermediates between depositors and borrowers."},"traps":["A bond is not an ownership share.","A mutual fund is an intermediary, not a single corporate bond.","Diversification spreads holdings; it does not promise zero risk or a guaranteed return."],"source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 3 and 5–6; Problem Set Ch26.docx, questions 1–10; Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, questions 1–3 only."},{"title":"2. Saving versus macroeconomic investment","body":["Saving is income not used for taxes and current consumption. Households may place their saving in a bank, stocks, bonds or mutual funds.","In the national accounts, investment means spending on newly produced capital, such as equipment, factories and newly built houses. Buying an existing stock or bond is a financial transaction, rather than directly adding to GDP investment.","A household buying a newly built home counts as investment in this framework. Do not classify every household purchase as consumption or every everyday use of the word 'investment' as national-accounts investment."],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Classify three purchases","prompt":"A saver buys shares, a business buys a newly produced machine, and a household pays to build a new house.","steps":["The share purchase is a way of holding financial saving.","The new business machine is investment.","Construction of the new house is also investment."],"answer":"The new machine and new house add to national-accounts investment; the financial share purchase does not directly do so."},"traps":["Purchasing stock is not the same accounting category as building a factory.","A newly built house belongs under investment.","Saving and investment can be carried out by different people even when their economy-wide totals are equal."],"source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 3, 6 and 14–16."},{"title":"3. The saving identities","body":["Begin with the expenditure identity. A closed economy has no international trade, so net exports equal zero. Rearranging gives national saving equal to investment.","Private saving subtracts taxes and consumption from income. Public saving subtracts government purchases from tax revenue. Adding them cancels taxes and gives national saving.","A budget deficit makes public saving negative. National saving can still be positive if private saving is large enough. Keep all quantities in the same units before adding them.","The identity S = I used here requires the closed-economy assumption. For an open economy, do not simply drop net exports from the accounting identity."],"formulas":[{"label":"Expenditure identity","expression":"Y = C + I + G + NX","note":"For a closed economy, NX = 0."},{"label":"Private saving","expression":"S_private = Y − T − C","note":"Income left after taxes and consumption."},{"label":"Public saving","expression":"S_public = T − G","note":"Negative when the government runs a deficit."},{"label":"National saving","expression":"S = S_private + S_public = Y − C − G","note":"Taxes cancel when the two saving components are added."},{"label":"Closed-economy investment","expression":"I = S","note":"Use only with the closed-economy assumption."},{"label":"Budget balance","expression":"Surplus = T − G; deficit = G − T","note":"A positive deficit amount corresponds to negative public saving."}],"example":{"title":"One table, all five answers","prompt":"A closed economy has Y = 2,000, C = 1,300, T = 400 and G = 500, all in billions. Find private saving, public saving, national saving, investment and the deficit.","steps":["Private saving = 2,000 − 400 − 1,300 = 300.","Public saving = 400 − 500 = −100.","National saving = 300 + (−100) = 200; check: 2,000 − 1,300 − 500 = 200.","Investment = national saving = 200.","Deficit = 500 − 400 = 100."],"answer":"Private saving 300; public saving −100; national saving and investment 200 each; deficit 100, all in billions."},"traps":["National saving subtracts government purchases, not taxes: Y − C − G.","A deficit of 100 means public saving is −100, not +100.","A deficit does not imply private saving is negative.","Taxes are not a separate component of GDP expenditure."],"source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 7–11 and 27; Problem Set Ch26.docx, questions 11–19; Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, questions 4–11 only. Numerical example is new practice."},{"title":"4. Tax changes: follow consumption too","body":["If income, consumption and government purchases are fixed, raising taxes reduces private saving and increases public saving by equal amounts. National saving stays unchanged because taxes cancel from Y − C − G.","For a tax cut with income and government purchases fixed, public saving falls by the amount of the cut. What happens to national saving depends on how much of the tax cut households spend.","If households save the entire tax cut, the increase in private saving offsets the fall in public saving. If households spend part of the cut, national saving falls by that increase in consumption. In a closed economy, investment falls by the same amount."],"formulas":[{"label":"National-saving change","expression":"ΔS = −ΔC when Y and G are fixed","note":"A tax change alone is not enough information unless household consumption behavior is specified."}],"example":{"title":"A tax cut is partly spent","prompt":"Taxes fall by 80 billion. Households spend 60 billion and save 20 billion of the cut. Income and government purchases stay fixed.","steps":["Public saving falls by 80 billion because T falls.","Private saving rises by 20 billion: the 80-billion tax reduction is partly offset by 60 billion more consumption.","National saving changes by +20 − 80 = −60 billion.","Closed-economy investment also falls by 60 billion."],"answer":"Public saving −80; private saving +20; national saving and investment −60 billion each."},"traps":["Do not automatically say a tax cut leaves national saving unchanged.","Distinguish the amount of the tax cut from the amount households spend.","State which variables are held fixed."],"source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 12–13; Problem Set Ch26.docx, question 16. Numerical example is new practice."},{"title":"5. Reading the loanable-funds market","body":["Saving supplies loanable funds; borrowing for investment creates demand. Put the real interest rate on the vertical axis and the quantity of loanable funds on the horizontal axis.","A higher interest rate encourages a larger quantity supplied and reduces the quantity demanded. Equilibrium is where the two quantities match. At a rate below equilibrium there is a shortage; at a rate above equilibrium there is a surplus.","A change in the interest rate itself produces movement along a curve. A change in saving incentives, investment incentives or public saving shifts a curve. Always identify the cause before naming the diagram movement."],"formulas":[{"label":"Equilibrium","expression":"Quantity supplied = quantity demanded; S = I","note":"This chapter uses a single loanable-funds market for the closed economy."}],"example":{"title":"Find the interest-rate pressure","prompt":"At the current interest rate, desired investment is 500 billion but saving supplied is only 400 billion.","steps":["Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied by 100 billion.","This is a shortage of loanable funds.","The interest rate tends to rise, encouraging saving and discouraging some investment until the market clears."],"answer":"A shortage creates upward pressure on the interest rate."},"traps":["The supply of funds comes from saving, not investment.","An interest-rate increase changes quantity supplied along the curve; it does not by itself shift supply.","A shortage is excess demand, not excess supply."],"source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 15–19 and 28; Problem Set Ch26.docx, questions 20–22. Numerical example is new practice."},{"title":"6. Policy shifts and crowding out","body":["A saving incentive shifts the supply curve right. The equilibrium interest rate falls and the quantity of loanable funds and investment rises, holding the demand curve fixed.","An investment incentive, such as an investment tax credit, shifts demand right. The equilibrium interest rate and quantity of funds rise, holding supply fixed.","A larger government deficit reduces public saving and national saving, shifting supply left in the course's model. The interest rate rises and private investment falls. This reduction in investment is crowding out.","A larger budget surplus has the opposite effect: supply shifts right, the interest rate falls and investment rises. Reduced capital formation can also weaken future productivity and output growth."],"formulas":[{"label":"Saving incentive","expression":"Supply → right; interest rate ↓; investment ↑","note":"Demand is held fixed."},{"label":"Investment incentive","expression":"Demand → right; interest rate ↑; investment ↑","note":"Supply is held fixed."},{"label":"Larger budget deficit","expression":"Public saving ↓; supply → left; interest rate ↑; investment ↓","note":"This is the supply-of-national-saving representation used in the slides."}],"example":{"title":"Explain the chain, not just the arrow","prompt":"Government purchases rise while taxes and private saving are unchanged. What does the loanable-funds model predict?","steps":["Public saving T − G falls.","National saving falls, so the supply of loanable funds shifts left.","The equilibrium interest rate rises.","At the higher interest rate, some private investment is crowded out."],"answer":"A larger deficit raises the interest rate and reduces private investment, other things equal."},"traps":["In these slides a deficit shifts the supply curve left; use that representation consistently.","Saving and investment incentives both raise investment but move the interest rate in opposite directions.","Crowding out refers to reduced private investment, not a claim that all private investment disappears."],"source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 21–28; Problem Set Ch26.docx, questions 23–26; Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 12 only."}],"questions":[{"id":"ch26-q1","chapter":"26","prompt":"Which arrangement makes the buyer a creditor rather than a part owner?","options":["Buying common stock","Buying a company's newly issued bond","Buying an equity mutual fund for ownership exposure","Receiving a share of ownership"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A bond is a certificate of indebtedness. A stock represents equity ownership.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slide 5; Problem Set Ch26.docx, questions 2–3 and 6."},{"id":"ch26-q2","chapter":"26","prompt":"A closed economy has Y = 900, C = 550, T = 180 and G = 220. Which saving calculation is correct?","options":["Private 170, public −40, national 130","Private 350, public 40, national 390","Private 130, public 40, national 170","Private 170, public 40, national 210"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Private saving = 900 − 180 − 550 = 170. Public saving = 180 − 220 = −40. National saving = 170 − 40 = 130, which also equals Y − C − G.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 7–11; Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, questions 4–11. Adapted numbers."},{"id":"ch26-q3","chapter":"26","prompt":"In a closed economy, Y, C and G are fixed. If taxes rise by 30, what happens?","options":["Private and national saving both rise by 30","Public saving falls by 30 and national saving is unchanged","Private saving falls by 30, public saving rises by 30 and national saving is unchanged","National saving falls by 60"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Taxes enter private and public saving with opposite signs. When Y, C and G remain fixed, national saving Y − C − G does not change.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 7–9; Problem Set Ch26.docx, question 16."},{"id":"ch26-q4","chapter":"26","prompt":"Which purchase counts as investment in the national accounts?","options":["Buying an existing share of stock","Depositing money into an existing bank account","Buying an existing government bond","Paying for a newly built house"],"correct":3,"explanation":"New residential construction adds to produced capital. The other choices are financial transactions or saving arrangements, not direct purchases of newly produced capital.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slide 14."},{"id":"ch26-q5","chapter":"26","prompt":"An interest-rate increase, with the saving supply curve otherwise unchanged, causes:","options":["A larger quantity of loanable funds supplied along the curve","A leftward shift of the saving supply curve","A rightward shift of the saving supply curve","A smaller quantity of loanable funds supplied"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A change in the variable on the vertical axis produces movement along the supply curve. Higher interest makes saving more attractive.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slide 17; Problem Set Ch26.docx, question 20."},{"id":"ch26-q6","chapter":"26","prompt":"Other things equal, a larger government budget deficit causes:","options":["Supply right, a lower interest rate and more investment","Supply left, a higher interest rate and less investment","Demand left, a lower interest rate and less investment","No change in national saving"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The deficit reduces public saving and national saving. With less loanable-funds supply, the equilibrium interest rate rises and investment is crowded out.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 23–28; Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 12."},{"id":"ch26-q7","chapter":"26","prompt":"Which change can lower the equilibrium interest rate while increasing investment?","options":["A larger government budget deficit","An investment tax credit that increases demand for funds","A saving incentive that shifts the supply of funds right","A decline in the supply of saving"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Greater supply lowers the equilibrium interest rate and raises the equilibrium quantity. An investment-demand increase raises both the interest rate and investment.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 21–22; Problem Set Ch26.docx, question 25."},{"id":"ch26-q8","chapter":"26","prompt":"Taxes are cut by 100. Households save 40 and spend 60 of the cut. Holding Y and G fixed, national saving in a closed economy:","options":["Rises by 100","Rises by 40","Stays unchanged","Falls by 60"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Public saving falls by 100 and private saving rises by 40. National saving therefore changes by −100 + 40 = −60; equivalently, consumption rises by 60 in S = Y − C − G.","source":"Chapter26.pptx, slides 12–13. Adapted numbers."}]}],"groups":[{"id":"review-a","title":"Review 23–24–25","scope":"All 30 questions","problems":[{"id":"a1","number":"1","chapter":"23","title":"What GDP counts","prompt":"Choose the best description of GDP.","choices":["The value of goods only","The value of services only","The market value of final goods and services produced within a country","The value of intermediate goods"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["GDP values final production within the country's borders during a specified period.","Both goods and services are included."],"answer":"C — Market value of final goods and services produced domestically.","trap":"The full definition also specifies a time period; intermediate goods are not counted separately.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 1"},{"id":"a2","number":"2","chapter":"23","title":"Current production","prompt":"Which transaction contributes to current GDP?","choices":["An existing laptop resold online","A newly produced car","Unpaid household work","Money received as a gift"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["A new car is current production.","Reselling a used item is not new production; an unpaid household service and a gift do not purchase new output."],"answer":"B — The newly produced car.","trap":"Any newly provided dealer service on a used good can count, but the used good's resale value does not.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 2"},{"id":"a3","number":"3","chapter":"23","title":"Nominal GDP from two goods","prompt":"An economy produces 100 phones at $500 each and 50 tablets at $400 each. Find nominal GDP.","choices":["$70,000","$80,000","$90,000","$100,000"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["Phones: 100 × $500 = $50,000.","Tablets: 50 × $400 = $20,000.","Add current-price values: $50,000 + $20,000 = $70,000."],"answer":"A — $70,000.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 3","table":{"headers":["Good","Quantity","Current price","Value"],"rows":[["Phones","100","$500","$50,000"],["Tablets","50","$400","$20,000"]]}},{"id":"a4","number":"4","chapter":"23","title":"Exports and GDP","prompt":"Exports rise while imports stay fixed. What happens to GDP, holding the other spending components fixed?","choices":["It falls","It is unchanged","It rises","It becomes zero"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["GDP = C + I + G + (exports − imports).","Higher exports raise net exports when imports are unchanged."],"answer":"C — GDP rises.","trap":"The conclusion holds other components constant.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 4"},{"id":"a5","number":"5","chapter":"23","title":"GDP deflator","prompt":"Nominal GDP is $600 billion and real GDP is $500 billion. Find the GDP deflator.","choices":["83","100","120","150"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["GDP deflator = (nominal GDP ÷ real GDP) × 100.","(600 ÷ 500) × 100 = 120."],"answer":"C — 120.","trap":"Do not reverse the ratio or omit ×100.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 5"},{"id":"a6","number":"6","chapter":"23","title":"Real GDP with constant prices","prompt":"Nominal GDP rises from $1,000 to $1,100 while the GDP deflator is unchanged. What happens to real GDP?","choices":["It rises","It falls","It is unchanged","There is insufficient information"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["Real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100.","With the deflator fixed, real GDP changes in the same proportion as nominal GDP.","Nominal growth = (1,100 − 1,000) ÷ 1,000 = 10%."],"answer":"A — Real GDP rises by 10%.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 6"},{"id":"a7","number":"7","chapter":"23","title":"Transfer payments","prompt":"How are government transfer payments treated in GDP?","choices":["They are consumption","They are investment","They are excluded as transfers","They are government purchases"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["A transfer redistributes income without buying a currently produced good or service.","GDP includes government purchases of output, not transfer payments themselves."],"answer":"C — The transfer payment itself is excluded.","trap":"A recipient's later spending on current production can count; the transfer is not counted a second time.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 7"},{"id":"a8","number":"8","chapter":"23","title":"Expenditure identity","prompt":"Given C = 400, I = 150, G = 200, exports = 80 and imports = 60, find GDP.","choices":["690","710","770","830"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["Net exports = 80 − 60 = 20.","GDP = 400 + 150 + 200 + 20 = 770."],"answer":"C — 770.","trap":"Subtract imports rather than adding them.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 8"},{"id":"a9","number":"9","chapter":"23","title":"Recover real GDP","prompt":"Nominal GDP is $900 and the GDP deflator is 150. Find real GDP.","choices":["450","600","750","900"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Real GDP = (nominal GDP ÷ deflator) × 100.","(900 ÷ 150) × 100 = 600."],"answer":"B — $600.","trap":"A deflator of 150 means prices are 1.5 times base-year prices, not 150 times.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 9"},{"id":"a10","number":"10","chapter":"24","title":"What CPI measures","prompt":"What is the purpose of the CPI?","choices":["Measure changes in total output","Track the cost of living over time","Measure productivity","Measure wages"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["CPI tracks the price of a representative consumer basket.","It helps measure changes in the cost of living."],"answer":"B — The cost of living over time.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 10"},{"id":"a11","number":"11","chapter":"24","title":"Inflation from an index","prompt":"CPI is 120 in 2023 and 132 in 2024. Find inflation in 2024.","choices":["10%","11%","12%","15%"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["Inflation = [(current CPI − previous CPI) ÷ previous CPI] × 100.","[(132 − 120) ÷ 120] × 100 = 10%."],"answer":"A — 10%.","trap":"The index rose 12 points, which is not a 12% increase.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 11"},{"id":"a12","number":"12","chapter":"24","title":"Consumer basket","prompt":"Which purchases belong in the CPI basket?","choices":["All exports","All goods purchased by firms","Goods and services purchased by consumers","All government spending"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["The CPI follows a representative consumer's purchases.","Investment and government purchases are not included merely because they are part of GDP."],"answer":"C — Consumer goods and services.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 12"},{"id":"a13","number":"13","chapter":"24","title":"Calculate CPI","prompt":"A fixed basket costs $200 in the base year and $260 this year. Find this year's CPI.","choices":["110","120","130","150"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["CPI = (current basket cost ÷ base-year basket cost) × 100.","(260 ÷ 200) × 100 = 130."],"answer":"C — 130.","trap":"The price level is 30% above the base year; that is not automatically this year's annual inflation.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 13"},{"id":"a14","number":"14","chapter":"24","title":"Substitution bias","prompt":"What behavior creates substitution bias in a fixed-basket CPI?","choices":["Ignoring changes in prices","Changes in product quality","Switching toward relatively cheaper products","Changing the CPI every year"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["When relative prices change, consumers can substitute toward cheaper alternatives.","A fixed basket does not fully reflect that response, so it can overstate the increase in the cost of living."],"answer":"C — Switching toward relatively cheaper goods.","trap":"Quality-change bias is a different problem.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 14"},{"id":"a15","number":"15","chapter":"24","title":"Real interest rate","prompt":"CPI rises from 100 to 105 and the nominal interest rate is 8%. Find the approximate real interest rate.","choices":["3%","5%","8%","13%"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["Inflation = [(105 − 100) ÷ 100] × 100 = 5%.","Approximate real interest = nominal interest − inflation = 8% − 5% = 3%."],"answer":"A — Approximately 3%.","trap":"The course uses the subtraction approximation. The exact rate is (1.08 ÷ 1.05 − 1) × 100 ≈ 2.86%.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 15"},{"id":"a16","number":"16","chapter":"24","title":"Inflation with a different base","prompt":"CPI increases from 200 in 2020 to 220 in 2021. Calculate inflation.","choices":["5%","10%","15%","20%"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Inflation = [(220 − 200) ÷ 200] × 100.","20 ÷ 200 × 100 = 10%."],"answer":"B — 10%.","trap":"A 20-point increase from an index of 200 is 10%, not 20%.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 16"},{"id":"a17","number":"17","chapter":"24","title":"Imports in price indices","prompt":"Which index can include imported consumer goods?","choices":["GDP deflator","CPI","Both","Neither"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["CPI can cover imported goods bought by domestic consumers.","The GDP deflator covers domestically produced final output, so imports are excluded."],"answer":"B — CPI.","trap":"CPI does not include every import; the import must fall within consumer purchases.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 17"},{"id":"a18","number":"18","chapter":"24","title":"Real salary growth","prompt":"Salary increases from $50,000 to $52,000 while prices rise 4%. Find real salary growth.","choices":["0%","1%","2%","4%"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["Nominal salary growth = [(52,000 − 50,000) ÷ 50,000] × 100 = 4%.","Real salary growth = (1.04 ÷ 1.04 − 1) × 100 = 0%."],"answer":"A — 0%; purchasing power is unchanged.","trap":"A larger salary does not guarantee greater purchasing power.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 18"},{"id":"a19","number":"19","chapter":"24","title":"Indexation","prompt":"What does indexation mean?","choices":["Fixing all prices","Adjusting payments for inflation","Controlling inflation","Measuring GDP"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Indexation links a monetary payment to a price index.","The adjustment helps protect purchasing power as prices change."],"answer":"B — Adjusting payments for inflation.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 19"},{"id":"a20","number":"20","chapter":"25","title":"Productivity definition","prompt":"Choose the productivity measure used by this question.","choices":["Output per worker","Total GDP","Inflation","Total investment"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["Productivity is output per unit of labor input.","Output per worker is one version; output per worker-hour is another."],"answer":"A — Output per worker.","trap":"Always match the denominator to the requested unit: workers or worker-hours.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 20"},{"id":"a21","number":"21","chapter":"25","title":"Output per worker","prompt":"A firm produces 600 units with 10 workers. Find output per worker.","choices":["6","60","600","6,000"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Output per worker = output ÷ number of workers.","600 ÷ 10 = 60."],"answer":"B — 60 units per worker.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 21"},{"id":"a22","number":"22","chapter":"25","title":"Human capital","prompt":"What is human capital?","choices":["Machines","Education and skills","Natural resources","Buildings"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Human capital is the knowledge and skills workers acquire through education, training and experience.","Machines and buildings are physical capital."],"answer":"B — Education and skills.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 22"},{"id":"a23","number":"23","chapter":"25","title":"Check the productivity unit","prompt":"Output is 10,000 units, with 200 workers working 10 hours per day. The sheet asks for 'productivity per day'; the choices imply output per worker-hour.","choices":["2","5","10","20"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Total labor input per day = 200 × 10 = 2,000 worker-hours.","If 10,000 is daily output, output per worker-hour = 10,000 ÷ 2,000 = 5.","Daily output per worker would instead be 10,000 ÷ 200 = 50; that value is absent from the choices."],"answer":"B — 5 units per worker-hour is the likely intended answer; the source's 'per day' wording is inconsistent.","trap":"Do not label 5 as units per worker per day. State the unit and the assumption about the output period.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 23","source_issue":"Ambiguous unit: the source asks 'productivity per day' but supplies choices matching productivity per worker-hour. Output's time period is not explicitly stated.","scoring_note":"Treat B as the likely intended choice, with the unit correction visible."},{"id":"a24","number":"24","chapter":"25","title":"Saving and future output","prompt":"In the growth model, what does a higher saving rate tend to support?","choices":["More consumption today","Less investment","More capital and higher future output","Lower productivity"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["More saving makes resources available for investment rather than current consumption.","More capital per worker raises productivity and the future level of output, other things equal."],"answer":"C — More capital and higher future output.","trap":"Diminishing returns mean a higher saving rate does not permanently raise the growth rate.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 24"},{"id":"a25","number":"25","chapter":"25","title":"Compare productivity","prompt":"Country X produces 20,000 units with 500 workers; Country Y produces 30,000 with 1,000 workers. Which has greater output per worker?","choices":["X","Y","They are equal","Cannot be determined"],"correct_choice":"a","correct_index":0,"steps":["X: 20,000 ÷ 500 = 40 units per worker.","Y: 30,000 ÷ 1,000 = 30 units per worker.","40 > 30, so X is more productive by this measure."],"answer":"A — Country X.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 25","table":{"headers":["Country","Output","Workers","Output per worker"],"rows":[["X","20,000","500","40"],["Y","30,000","1,000","30"]]}},{"id":"a26","number":"26","chapter":"25","title":"Diminishing returns","prompt":"What are diminishing returns to capital?","choices":["More capital always reduces output","Each additional unit of capital adds less extra output","Productivity always rises at the same rate","Output never changes"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Hold other production inputs and technology fixed.","As capital increases, an additional unit produces a smaller increase in output than the previous unit."],"answer":"B — Each extra unit of capital adds less output.","trap":"Output can still rise; it rises by smaller increments.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 26"},{"id":"a27","number":"27","chapter":"25","title":"Recognize diminishing returns","prompt":"Capital rises from 10 to 20 and output from 100 to 120. Capital then rises to 30 and output to 130. What pattern is shown?","choices":["Increasing returns","Diminishing returns","Constant returns","No returns"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["First 10-unit capital increase adds 20 units of output.","Second 10-unit capital increase adds only 10 units of output.","The extra output from equal increments of capital is falling."],"answer":"B — Diminishing returns to capital.","trap":"This is about marginal returns to one input, not returns to scale when all inputs change.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 27"},{"id":"a28","number":"28","chapter":"25","title":"Foreign direct investment","prompt":"Which benefit of foreign direct investment fits the growth model?","choices":["Lower productivity","Technology transfer","Lower wages","Less capital"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["FDI brings foreign-owned and operated productive capital.","It can also introduce technology and production knowledge."],"answer":"B — It can increase technology transfer.","trap":"Technology transfer is a potential benefit, not a guarantee for every investment.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 28"},{"id":"a29","number":"29","chapter":"25","title":"Growth per person","prompt":"Real GDP per person rises from $20,000 to $21,000. Calculate the growth rate.","choices":["1%","2%","5%","10%"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["Growth = [(21,000 − 20,000) ÷ 20,000] × 100.","1,000 ÷ 20,000 × 100 = 5%."],"answer":"C — 5%.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 29"},{"id":"a30","number":"30","chapter":"25","title":"Growth-supporting policies","prompt":"Which listed policy supports long-run growth in the course model?","choices":["Inward-oriented trade restrictions","Higher barriers to foreign investment","Reduced education subsidies","None of these"],"correct_choice":"d","correct_index":3,"steps":["The course supports openness to trade and investment, education, and incentives for productive investment.","The first three options move against those channels."],"answer":"D — None of the listed policies.","trap":"Growth policies include education, saving and investment, property rights, openness, and research incentives.","source":"Review Chapters 23-24-25.docx, question 30"}]},{"id":"review-b","title":"Review 26–29–30","scope":"Questions 1–12 only (Chapter 26)","problems":[{"id":"b1","number":"1","chapter":"26","title":"Financial system's role","prompt":"What is the financial system's main role?","choices":["Reduce government spending","Connect savers with borrowers","Control inflation","Increase exports"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Savers provide funds; borrowers use funds for investment.","The financial system brings these sides together."],"answer":"B — Connect savers with borrowers.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 1"},{"id":"b2","number":"2","chapter":"26","title":"Equity versus debt","prompt":"A firm sells ownership shares to raise finance. Which type of finance is this?","choices":["Debt finance","Equity finance","Retained earnings","Indirect finance"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["A share is an ownership claim in the firm.","Raising money by issuing shares is equity finance."],"answer":"B — Equity finance.","trap":"A bond represents debt; a share represents ownership.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 2"},{"id":"b3","number":"3","chapter":"26","title":"Financial intermediary","prompt":"Which listed institution is a financial intermediary?","choices":["A corporate bond","A mutual fund","A Treasury bill","A share of stock"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["A mutual fund pools savers' money and invests it in assets.","The other options are financial instruments, not intermediary institutions."],"answer":"B — A mutual fund.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 3"},{"id":"b4","number":"4","chapter":"26","title":"Saving equals investment","prompt":"In a closed economy, national saving equals what?","choices":["Consumption","Investment","Government purchases","Net exports"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["A closed economy has NX = 0, so Y = C + I + G.","Rearrange: Y − C − G = I.","National saving is S = Y − C − G, so S = I."],"answer":"B — Investment.","trap":"S = I here relies on the closed-economy national-income identity.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 4"},{"id":"b5","number":"5","chapter":"26","title":"National saving formula","prompt":"Choose the national saving identity for a closed economy.","choices":["S = Y − C","S = Y − T − C","S = Y − C − G","S = T − G"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["Add private saving, Y − T − C, and public saving, T − G.","Taxes cancel: S = Y − C − G."],"answer":"C — S = Y − C − G.","trap":"Y − T − C is private saving; T − G is public saving.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 5"},{"id":"b6","number":"6","chapter":"26","title":"Public saving formula","prompt":"Which expression defines public saving?","choices":["Y − C − T","T − G","Y − C − G","I − S"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Public saving is tax revenue left after government purchases.","Therefore public saving = T − G."],"answer":"B — T − G.","trap":"The positive size of a budget deficit is G − T; public saving is its negative.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 6"},{"id":"b7","number":"7","chapter":"26","title":"Deficit and public saving","prompt":"What does a government budget deficit imply?","choices":["Public saving is positive","Public saving is zero","Public saving is negative","Private saving must be negative"],"correct_choice":"c","correct_index":2,"steps":["A deficit means government purchases exceed taxes: G > T.","Therefore public saving, T − G, is negative."],"answer":"C — Public saving is negative.","trap":"A public deficit alone does not tell you the sign of private saving.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 7"},{"id":"b8","number":"8","chapter":"26","title":"Calculate national saving","prompt":"Given Y = 800, C = 500 and G = 200, calculate national saving.","choices":["50","100","150","200"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["S = Y − C − G.","S = 800 − 500 − 200 = 100."],"answer":"B — 100.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 8"},{"id":"b9","number":"9","chapter":"26","title":"Calculate private saving","prompt":"Given Y = 900, C = 600 and T = 150, calculate private saving.","choices":["100","150","200","250"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Private saving = Y − T − C.","900 − 150 − 600 = 150."],"answer":"B — 150.","trap":"Subtract taxes, not government purchases, when calculating private saving.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 9"},{"id":"b10","number":"10","chapter":"26","title":"Calculate public saving","prompt":"Taxes are T = 300 and government purchases are G = 350. Find public saving and the budget position.","choices":["+50; surplus","−50; deficit","+50; deficit","−50; surplus"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["Public saving = T − G = 300 − 350 = −50.","Government spending exceeds taxes by 50, so the deficit's size is 50."],"answer":"B — Public saving is −50; the government has a deficit of 50.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 10"},{"id":"b11","number":"11","chapter":"26","title":"Positive national saving","prompt":"If national saving is positive in a closed economy, which statement follows?","choices":["Y − C − G > 0","Investment is positive","At least one of private or public saving is positive","All of these"],"correct_choice":"d","correct_index":3,"steps":["S > 0 directly implies Y − C − G > 0.","Since S = I in a closed economy, I > 0.","Since S is private plus public saving, they cannot both be zero or negative if their sum is positive."],"answer":"D — All three statements follow.","trap":"Positive national saving does not require both private and public saving to be positive.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 11"},{"id":"b12","number":"12","chapter":"26","title":"Deficit and loanable funds","prompt":"Holding other factors fixed, what does a larger government deficit do in the course's loanable-funds model?","choices":["Increases the supply of loanable funds","Decreases the supply of loanable funds","Decreases demand for loanable funds","Leaves the market unchanged"],"correct_choice":"b","correct_index":1,"steps":["A larger deficit lowers public saving.","With private saving unchanged, national saving falls.","The supply curve shifts left; the equilibrium interest rate rises and investment falls."],"answer":"B — The supply of loanable funds decreases.","trap":"This course represents the deficit as a leftward supply shift; distinguish a curve shift from a movement along it.","source":"Review Chapters 26-29-30.docx, question 12"}]}],"flashcards":[{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f01","front":"What does GDP measure?","back":"The market value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a given period. Location of production matters, not the owner's nationality."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f02","front":"Why does total expenditure equal total income?","back":"A buyer's spending becomes income earned from production. They are two ways of measuring the same activity, so do not add them together."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f03","front":"Why are intermediate goods excluded from GDP?","back":"Their value is already included in the final product. Counting both would double count production. The same good can be final or intermediate depending on its use."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f04","front":"Does selling a used car count in current GDP?","back":"The old car's resale value does not: it was produced earlier. Newly produced replacement parts or a newly supplied dealer service can count separately."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f05","front":"Does unpaid household work count in measured GDP?","back":"Generally no. Exclusion from measured GDP does not mean the work has no value."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f06","front":"What belongs in consumption, C?","back":"Household purchases of goods and services, including durable goods. A newly built house is the important exception: it belongs in investment."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f07","front":"What belongs in GDP investment, I?","back":"New capital equipment, structures, housing and inventory changes. Buying stocks or bonds is a financial transaction, not GDP investment."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f08","front":"What belongs in government purchases, G?","back":"Government purchases of current goods and services, including employees' services and public works. Transfer payments are excluded because no current product is exchanged."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f09","front":"What is the expenditure identity?","back":"Y = C + I + G + NX, where NX = exports − imports. Net exports can be negative."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f10","front":"Why are imports subtracted in GDP?","back":"Imported goods can already appear in C, I or G. Subtracting imports removes foreign production. An imported machine raises I and lowers NX by the same amount."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f11","front":"How is nominal GDP calculated?","back":"Add current-year price × current-year quantity for each final product. Nominal GDP can rise because prices rise, output rises, or both."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f12","front":"How is real GDP calculated?","back":"Add base-year price × current-year quantity for each final product. Keep prices fixed, not quantities, to compare production over time."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f13","front":"What is the GDP deflator?","back":"GDP deflator = (nominal GDP ÷ real GDP) × 100. In the base year it is 100. A value of 120 means prices are 20% above the base-year level."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f14","front":"How do you recover real GDP from nominal GDP and the deflator?","back":"Real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ (GDP deflator ÷ 100). If the deflator is 125, divide nominal GDP by 1.25."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f15","front":"How do you calculate real GDP growth?","back":"[(New real GDP − old real GDP) ÷ old real GDP] × 100%. Use the old value in the denominator. Higher nominal GDP alone does not prove real growth."},{"chapter":"23","id":"ch23-f16","front":"Why is GDP per person an incomplete measure of well-being?","back":"Real GDP per person = real GDP ÷ population. It is an average; it does not directly measure distribution, leisure, unpaid work or environmental quality."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f01","front":"What does the CPI track?","back":"The overall cost of a typical consumer's basket of goods and services, including relevant imported consumer goods."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f02","front":"What stays fixed in a basic CPI calculation?","back":"The basket quantities. Price the same quantities each year so the comparison captures price changes."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f03","front":"What are the five steps for calculating CPI and inflation?","back":"Fix the basket → collect prices → calculate basket costs → choose a base year and calculate CPI → calculate the inflation rate."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f04","front":"What is the CPI formula?","back":"CPI = (current basket cost ÷ base-year basket cost) × 100. Base-year CPI = 100. A CPI of 130 means the basket costs 30% more than in the base year."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f05","front":"What is the inflation-rate formula?","back":"Inflation = [(new CPI − old CPI) ÷ old CPI] × 100%. The old CPI is the denominator."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f06","front":"CPI rises from 125 to 175. What is inflation?","back":"(175 − 125) ÷ 125 × 100 = 40%. The increase is 50 index points, not 50%."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f07","front":"How do you find the earlier CPI from inflation and the new CPI?","back":"Old CPI = new CPI ÷ (1 + inflation as a decimal). For 24% inflation, divide the new CPI by 1.24."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f08","front":"Which price index includes imported consumer goods?","back":"CPI includes relevant imported consumer goods. The GDP deflator excludes imported production because it measures domestic production."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f09","front":"Which index includes a domestically produced industrial tractor?","back":"The GDP deflator, because the tractor is domestic production. It is not part of the typical consumer basket, so it is excluded from CPI."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f10","front":"How do the CPI and GDP deflator differ in their baskets?","back":"Basic CPI fixes a consumer basket. The GDP deflator reflects the composition of current domestic production."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f11","front":"What is substitution bias?","back":"Consumers switch toward relatively cheaper goods, but a fixed basket misses part of that response. It tends to overstate increases in the cost of living."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f12","front":"What other two problems can make CPI overstate living-cost increases?","back":"New goods increase choice and purchasing power, while quality improvements add value. A fixed basket and imperfect quality adjustment miss some of these benefits."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f13","front":"How do you convert an old money amount to target-year purchasing power?","back":"Target-year amount = old amount × (target-year CPI ÷ old-year CPI). Both CPIs must use the same index series and base."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f14","front":"What is indexation?","back":"Automatic adjustment of a money amount for inflation by law or contract, such as an indexed wage or benefit."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f15","front":"What is the course formula for real interest?","back":"Real interest rate ≈ nominal interest rate − inflation rate. It is the course's approximation; use percentage rates, not CPI index levels."},{"chapter":"24","id":"ch24-f16","front":"Can a deposit earn positive interest but lose purchasing power?","back":"Yes. If nominal interest is 4% and inflation is 7%, the course's real rate is approximately −3%. The money balance rises, but purchasing power falls."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f01","front":"What is productivity?","back":"Output per unit of labor. Output per worker and output per labor-hour use different denominators, so read the units carefully."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f02","front":"Three workers each work eight hours and produce 1,200 units. What is hourly productivity?","back":"Total labor-hours = 3 × 8 = 24. Productivity = 1,200 ÷ 24 = 50 units per labor-hour."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f03","front":"How do you measure average material living standards?","back":"Real GDP per person = real GDP ÷ population. Convert to compatible units before dividing. Total GDP alone does not measure the average living standard."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f04","front":"What is physical capital?","back":"Produced equipment and structures used in production, such as machines, factory buildings and business computers."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f05","front":"What is human capital?","back":"Knowledge and skills embodied in people through education, training and experience. It is not simply the number of workers or hours worked."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f06","front":"What are natural resources?","back":"Production inputs supplied by nature, such as land and mineral deposits. They are one productivity determinant, not the only source of prosperity."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f07","front":"How is technological knowledge different from human capital?","back":"Technology is society's understanding of useful production methods. Human capital is a person's acquired knowledge and ability to apply those methods."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f08","front":"What does the production function Y = A F(L, K, H, N) mean?","back":"Output depends on labor, physical capital, human capital and natural resources, combined using technology A. Better technology allows more output from the same inputs."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f09","front":"What are constant returns to scale?","back":"Multiplying every input by the same factor multiplies output by that factor, holding technology fixed. Doubling all inputs doubles output."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f10","front":"What are diminishing returns to capital?","back":"As capital rises with other inputs fixed, output rises by progressively smaller amounts. This can coexist with constant returns to scale, which changes all inputs together."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f11","front":"Does a permanently higher saving rate permanently raise the growth rate?","back":"Not from capital accumulation alone in the course's model. Saving supports investment and a higher income level, but diminishing returns make the extra growth fade. Consumption is sacrificed initially."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f12","front":"What is the catch-up effect?","back":"Other things equal, capital-poor economies can grow faster because additional capital has a larger payoff when capital per worker is low. Catch-up is not automatic."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f13","front":"How do foreign direct and portfolio investment differ?","back":"Direct investment is foreign-owned and foreign-operated capital. Portfolio investment uses foreign financing with domestic operation."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f14","front":"How can policy raise productivity?","back":"Education and training raise human capital; secure property rights encourage investment; research improves technology. Education also has an opportunity cost."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f15","front":"What are the competing effects of population growth?","back":"It can dilute capital and stretch resources and education, but may also increase the number of potential scientists, engineers and inventors. Name the channel rather than calling it always good or bad."},{"chapter":"25","id":"ch25-f16","front":"When does capital per worker fall even though total capital rises?","back":"When labor grows faster than capital. For example, K/L falls from 200/100 = 2 to 220/125 = 1.76, although total capital increases."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f01","front":"How do financial markets and intermediaries channel saving?","back":"Financial markets, such as bond and stock markets, connect savers and borrowers directly. Intermediaries, such as banks and mutual funds, channel funds between them."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f02","front":"What is the difference between a bond and a stock?","back":"A bond is debt: the buyer is a creditor. A stock is equity: the buyer is a part owner whose return depends on the firm's success."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f03","front":"How is macroeconomic investment different from buying financial assets?","back":"Investment means purchases of newly produced capital, including new machines, factories and houses. Buying an existing stock or bond is a financial transaction, not direct GDP investment."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f04","front":"What is private saving?","back":"S_private = Y − T − C: income left after taxes and consumption."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f05","front":"What is public saving?","back":"S_public = T − G: tax revenue minus government purchases. It is negative during a budget deficit."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f06","front":"What is national saving?","back":"S = S_private + S_public = Y − C − G. Taxes cancel when private and public saving are added."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f07","front":"When does national saving equal investment?","back":"In a closed economy: NX = 0, so Y = C + I + G and S = Y − C − G = I. Do not drop NX without the closed-economy assumption."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f08","front":"A government has T = 400 and G = 500. What are its deficit and public saving?","back":"Deficit = G − T = 100. Public saving = T − G = −100. A positive deficit amount corresponds to negative public saving."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f09","front":"If taxes rise while Y, C and G stay fixed, what happens to saving?","back":"Private saving falls and public saving rises by equal amounts. National saving stays unchanged because S = Y − C − G."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f10","front":"Taxes fall by 100; households spend 60 and save 40 of the cut. What happens to national saving?","back":"With Y and G fixed, public saving falls by 100 and private saving rises by 40. National saving falls by 60; closed-economy investment also falls by 60."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f11","front":"What are the axes, supply and demand in the loanable-funds market?","back":"Vertical: real interest rate. Horizontal: quantity of loanable funds. Saving supplies funds; borrowing for investment creates demand. Equilibrium has S = I."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f12","front":"Does an interest-rate change shift a loanable-funds curve?","back":"No. By itself it causes movement along a curve: higher interest raises quantity supplied and reduces quantity demanded. Other causes can shift the curves."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f13","front":"What happens below and above the equilibrium interest rate?","back":"Below equilibrium: demand exceeds supply, creating a shortage and upward pressure on interest. Above equilibrium: supply exceeds demand, creating a surplus and downward pressure."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f14","front":"What happens when a saving incentive shifts supply right?","back":"Holding demand fixed, the equilibrium real interest rate falls and the quantity of loanable funds and investment rises."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f15","front":"What happens when an investment incentive shifts demand right?","back":"Holding supply fixed, the equilibrium real interest rate and quantity of loanable funds rise. Investment rises too."},{"chapter":"26","id":"ch26-f16","front":"How does a larger budget deficit crowd out investment in the course model?","back":"Public and national saving fall → loanable-funds supply shifts left → the real interest rate rises → private investment falls. Crowding out does not mean all investment disappears."}]};
